{
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"id\":\"DATA-2\",\"title\":\"Add const-safe field builders and align DataFn schema inference\",\"description\":\"## Executive summary\\n\\nAdd a public const-safe field-construction API to `@datafn/core` and align DataFn's inferred record types with its runtime field defaults. The API should make reusable schema fields as type-safe as inline literals passed directly to `defineSchema`, while replacing positional consumer helpers with named, type-appropriate options.\\n\\nNucleum's current DataFn schema demonstrates the need: it maintains near-duplicate const-generic helpers for each field kind, has inconsistent positional argument ordering, and must spread helper results to add enum metadata.\\n\\n## Problem or opportunity\\n\\n`defineSchema` uses a const generic and preserves literals for objects written directly at its call site. Literal safety becomes fragile when consumers extract reusable fields, annotate field collections with broad schema types, or write their own helper functions. A small generic mistake can widen field names, kinds, requiredness, nullability, or enum members, weakening generated client record types and compile-time index validation.\\n\\nDataFn's own client type tests currently recreate const-generic string and boolean helpers. Public examples commonly use inline objects or broad `DatafnSchema` annotations, which can erase the literal information the typed client needs.\\n\\nThere is also a current type/runtime mismatch for optional fields:\\n\\n* Runtime mutation validation treats omitted `nullable` as `false`.\\n* `DatafnResourceRecord` currently adds `null` unless the schema contains the literal `nullable: false`.\\n\\nA builder that emits explicit defaults would protect builder users, but DataFn's inference should also represent the runtime default correctly for inline schemas.\\n\\n## Expected outcome\\n\\nSchema authors can define reusable fields through a stable DataFn-owned API such as:\\n\\n```ts\\nfield.string(\\\"status\\\", {\\n  required: true,\\n  enum: [\\\"NOT_STARTED\\\", \\\"IN_PROGRESS\\\", \\\"COMPLETED\\\"]\\n});\\n\\nfield.boolean(\\\"isStarred\\\", {\\n  default: false\\n});\\n```\\n\\nThe returned values remain plain schema objects, preserve all inference-relevant literals, and use named options whose values match the selected field kind. Inline schemas and builder-based schemas infer equivalent client record types.\\n\\n## Goals\\n\\n* Add const-safe builders for every public DataFn field kind, including `file`.\\n* Preserve literal `name`, `type`, `required`, `nullable`, and enum members.\\n* Use named options rather than positional booleans and default values.\\n* Emit explicit default literals for omitted `required` and `nullable`, consistent with runtime semantics.\\n* Keep optionality and nullability independent.\\n* Type defaults, enums, bounds, patterns, uniqueness, encryption, volatility, and read-only metadata according to the supported field contract.\\n* Prevent options from overriding builder-owned `name` and `type`.\\n* Align `DatafnResourceRecord` nullable inference with runtime defaults for inline schemas as well as builder output.\\n* Update public examples to use `defineSchema` and/or the field API without broad annotations that erase literals.\\n* Add compile-time coverage for record inference and index-name validation.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n\\n* Introducing a stateful schema DSL or a second schema representation.\\n* Moving consumer-specific composite fields such as Nucleum labels or share-field bundles into DataFn.\\n* Preserving Nucleum's current positional helper signatures as an upstream compatibility API.\\n* Changing existing persisted schema semantics or automatically migrating consumer schemas.\\n* Claiming runtime enforcement for field constraints that DataFn does not currently enforce.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n* Required fields infer as required properties of the correct value type.\\n* Optional non-nullable fields infer as optional without `null`.\\n* Nullable fields infer `null` exactly where runtime validation permits it.\\n* Literal enum members narrow the inferred client value type.\\n* A misspelled index field is rejected at compile time when builders are used.\\n* Incompatible defaults, enum values, and type-specific options fail type checking.\\n* All current DataFn field kinds have supported construction paths.\\n* Builder output is a valid plain `DatafnFieldSchema` object and produces stable runtime schema behavior.\\n* Existing object-literal schemas remain source-compatible.\\n* Documentation explains when `defineSchema`, field builders, and explicit schema annotations preserve or erase literal inference.\\n* Nucleum can replace its generic local field factories with the public API without changing its effective schema; product-specific composites remain local.\\n* Type tests cover reusable field constants, shared field arrays, enums, required/optional/nullability combinations, and invalid indices.\\n\\n## Dependencies and risks\\n\\n* Public exports and schema types in `@datafn/core`.\\n* Client record inference in `datafn/client`.\\n* Core/client compile-time tests, examples, and documentation.\\n* A released DataFn version before downstream consumers should remove their local generic helpers.\\n* Risk of publishing a permissive options bag that accepts invalid combinations or misspelled keys.\\n* Risk of changing schema hashes or normalized output if builders emit properties differently from current literals.\\n* Risk of treating optional and nullable as equivalent.\\n* Risk of fixing builder output while leaving inline schema inference inconsistent.\\n* Constraint metadata must be described honestly where runtime enforcement is incomplete.\\n\\n## Open questions\\n\\n* Should the public surface use a namespace such as `field.string` or named exports such as `stringField`?\\n* Should DataFn provide a dedicated `field.id()`, given that the runtime privileges the `id` field, or leave exact ID defaults to consumers?\\n* Should builders reject every unknown option strictly, or preserve an extension mechanism for future/provider metadata?\\n* Should omitted-default normalization exist only in builders, in `defineSchema`'s type model, or in both?\\n* Which field constraints are type-specific today, and which require separate runtime-validation work before being promoted in the builder API?\",\"priority\":{\"value\":0,\"name\":\"No priority\"},\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/DATA-2/add-const-safe-field-builders-and-align-datafn-schema-inference\",\"gitBranchName\":\"aravink/data-2-add-const-safe-field-builders-and-align-datafn-schema\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-29T12:49:51.915Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-29T12:49:51.915Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"completedAt\":null,\"startedAt\":null,\"canceledAt\":null,\"dueDate\":null,\"slaStartedAt\":null,\"slaMediumRiskAt\":null,\"slaHighRiskAt\":null,\"slaBreachesAt\":null,\"status\":\"Backlog\",\"statusType\":\"backlog\",\"labels\":[],\"attachments\":[],\"documents\":[],\"createdBy\":\"Aravind\",\"createdById\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"project\":\"Super functions t1\",\"projectId\":\"ddb7a6a8-0c46-48f2-80c1-e4533bbe1750\",\"team\":\"Datafn\",\"teamId\":\"6a9a3cda-6a82-473d-b96c-157912a0a594\"}"
    }
  ]
}

